  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64268,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most are solitary and &lt;&nbsp;1&nbsp;cm {14010498}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:58:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64269,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Endocervical polyps are composed of fibrovascular tissue lined by benign endocervical-type epithelium, with glands permeating the central stroma {14010498}. There is commonly squamous metaplasia or microglandular change. Rarely, surface papillary proliferation or stromal decidual change may occur. The surface of polyps may be inflamed or eroded and associated with reactive/reparative changes. Squamous intraepithelial lesion may be present {18753960}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:58:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64270,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Reactive or reparative changes can be seen in the setting of an endocervical polyp. Repair in squamous cells is characterized by monolayer sheets with round to oval nuclei and smooth nuclear contours. Nucleoli may be present, and streaming can be seen but is less apparent in liquid-based preparations. Reactive endocervical cells are notable for mild nuclear enlargement and slight crowding but have round to oval nuclei with smooth nuclear contour and even chromatin. Marked reactive changes in a polyp can lead to a cytological diagnosis of atypical squamous cells, atypical endocervical cells, or atypical glandular cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:58:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64271,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:58:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64272,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> fibrovascular connective tissue lined by benign endocervical-type mucinous epithelium, often with squamous metaplasia; glands are typically also present within the polyp stroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:58:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64273,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:58:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64274,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 291,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Endocervical polyps are benign lesions, although they can rarely be involved by in situ lesions or invasive carcinomas, either solely or as part of more-generalized cervical neoplasia {18753960}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T14:58:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]